'''Nefamese''' or '''''Arunamese''''' is a [[pidgin]] of [[Arunachal Pradesh]] (formerly [[North-East Frontier Agency|NEFA]]), India. Its classification is unclear; ''Ethnologue'' states that it is based on the [[Assamese language]], but also that it is most closely related to the Sino-Tibetan [[Gallong language|Gallong]] like the Assamese language formed out by the mixture of languages like [[Austroasiatic languages|Austroasiatic]], [[Tibeto-Burman]], [[Tai languages|Tai]] and [[Indo-European languages|Indo-European]] family of languages. 

Nefamese emerged in eastern Arunachal Pradesh as a [[lingua franca]] among the [[Nyishi people|Nyishi]], [[Adi people|Adi]], [[Apatani people|Apatanai]], [[Khampti]], [[Hill Miri people|Hill Miri]], [[Idu Mishmi language|Idu Mishimi]], [[Nocte people|Nocte]], [[Wancho Naga|Wancho]], [[Tagin people|Tagin]], [[Monpa people|Mompa]], [[Zekhring people|Zakhring]], and [[Bugun language|Bugun]] peoples, among others—between them and with other [[Assamese people|Indigenous Assamese people]] and other Indigenous groups of [[Northeast India]]. The language is threatened by, and has perhaps somewhat been replaced by, the use of [[Hindi]].

